1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,100
When the moon went dark
over ancient Assyria,

2
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:06,120
the king's scholars had
a way to save his life.

3
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:10,384
They put another man on his throne
— and when the danger had passed,

4
00:00:10,384 --> 00:00:13,280
they let that man die in the king's place.

5
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:17,940
To the people of Mesopotamia, the
stars were the writing of heaven,

6
00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:21,720
and the scholars of the palace
spent their nights reading it.

7
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:24,900
An eclipse was the darkest message of all.

8
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:27,700
They watched which part of
the moon the shadow covered,

9
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,620
because each part stood for a land.

10
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:34,680
If the shadow fell on the
king's land, the king would die.

11
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,900
But an omen was a warning, not a verdict.

12
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:41,380
And a warning could be redirected.

13
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:46,460
In the seventh century BCE,
Assyria was ruled by Esarhaddon.

14
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:51,100
Hundreds of letters from his scholars
survive, and they show how it was done.

15
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:54,980
When the shadow fell on his
lands, Esarhaddon left his throne.

16
00:00:54,980 --> 00:01:00,180
Another man sat on it, washed, anointed
and dressed in the king's robes,

17
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:02,960
with a young woman
beside him as his queen.

18
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:06,720
One scholar wrote: "I wrote
down whatever signs there were,

19
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:10,380
and had them recited in front
of Shamash," the sun god.

20
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:13,640
"The substitute king took
the signs on himself."

21
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:17,520
Another bound the written
omens into the hem of his robe.

22
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:20,260
The real king stayed hidden in his palace.

23
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:24,220
His scholars kept writing to
him, but no longer as king.

24
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:27,380
Their letters begin: "To
the farmer, my lord."

25
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,320
The substitute could sit on the
throne for up to a hundred days.

26
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:35,340
Then, in the words of the
letters, he "went to his fate."

27
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:39,080
The scholars preferred a man
whose death would trouble no one:

28
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:42,200
a condemned criminal,
a prisoner, a laborer.

29
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:47,180
But in 671 BCE, the substitute
was someone who mattered.

30
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:52,380
Damqi was the son of a high temple
official in the Babylonian city of Akkad.

31
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:55,740
The king's agent in
Babylonia reported his end.

32
00:01:56,020 --> 00:02:00,340
Damqi, "who had ruled Assyria,
Babylonia and all the countries,

33
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:03,780
died with his queen," as
a substitute for the king.

34
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,540
"He and his queen were decorated, treated,
displayed, buried and wailed over."

35
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:11,800
Then the report adds something else.

36
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:17,580
Before the ritual, a prophetess had told
Damqi: "You will take over the kingship!"

37
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:18,780
She was right.

38
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:22,960
Whether that prophecy is why he was
chosen, the letter does not say.

39
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:25,900
It says only that the
rituals "succeeded well

40
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:29,920
indeed," and that "the
king, my lord, can be glad."

41
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:34,900
The scholars carried on, as they wrote,
"as our fathers did for their lords."

42
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:41,480
In the summer of 669 BCE, they set the day
for another substitute to go to his fate.

43
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:47,260
That autumn, on the march to Egypt,
Esarhaddon fell ill, and died.

44
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:50,420
The ritual was far older than him.

45
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:54,280
Its earliest story comes
from a Babylonian chronicle,

46
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:59,700
and it is set more than a thousand years
before Esarhaddon, in the city of Isin.

47
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:04,220
There, the chronicle says, King
Erra-imitti made his gardener,

48
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:09,760
Enlil-bani, substitute king, and
placed the royal tiara on his head.

49
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:14,740
Then Erra-imitti died in his
own palace, sipping a hot soup.

50
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:18,740
The gardener, sitting on the
throne, did not give it up.

51
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:24,560
Enlil-bani ruled Isin for twenty-four
years, and his own inscriptions survive.

52
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:30,840
He rebuilt the city wall and named it
"Enlil-bani is firm as to foundation."

53
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:34,540
He cut the barley tax
from a fifth to a tenth.

54
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,720
Historians suspect the tale is a legend,

55
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:41,280
perhaps one that dressed up an
ordinary seizure of the throne.

56
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:46,180
But in every letter from Nineveh,
the ritual ends the same way:

57
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:50,860
the stand-in dies, and the
king lives, for a while.

58
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:54,180
Only the oldest story ends the other way.

59
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:56,160
The gods took the king.

60
00:03:56,340 --> 00:03:59,800
And the man crowned to die kept the crown.

61
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:04,741
A borrowed crown could buy a
king a hundred days — and once,

62
00:04:04,741 --> 00:04:07,460
it bought a gardener a kingdom.

63
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:11,560
For more strange rituals and
forgotten lives like this,

64
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,440
leave a like and subscribe
to Animated Tales.

65
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:15,713
Until next time.
